<p class="lead">If your flat roof is leaking in a few places, baking the top floor every summer and still has years of structure left, a tear-off may be more roof than you need. Austin commercial roof coatings from Austin Roofing seal the surface, reflect the Texas sun and buy you years of service for far less disruption than a full replacement.</p>

<p>We apply silicone, acrylic and polyurethane coating systems over TPO, EPDM, modified bitumen, built-up and metal roofs on warehouses, retail buildings, offices, churches and multi-family properties across the Austin area. The work starts the same way every time: finding out whether your roof is actually a good candidate.</p>

<h2>Is Your Roof a Good Candidate for a Coating?</h2>
<p>The honest answer to “should I coat my roof?” is “it depends on what is underneath.” A coating bonds to the surface it is applied over. If that surface is sound and the insulation below it is dry, a coating can perform for a long time. If the roof is saturated, the coating just traps the problem.</p>
<div class="pros-cons">
  <div class="pros"><h4>Good candidates</h4><ul>
    <li>Membrane or metal still well attached, with isolated leaks</li>
    <li>Surface cracking, chalking or granule loss from UV</li>
    <li>Dry insulation (confirmed by moisture survey or cores)</li>
    <li>Owners who want lower cooling loads on a hot roof</li>
    <li>Buildings where a tear-off would disrupt tenants or operations</li>
  </ul></div>
  <div class="cons"><h4>Poor candidates</h4><ul>
    <li>Wet or crushed insulation across large areas</li>
    <li>Soft, rotted or rusted-through decking</li>
    <li>Membrane that is shrinking, splitting or detached</li>
    <li>Serious structural ponding the coating cannot correct</li>
    <li>Roofs already at the maximum number of layers code allows</li>
  </ul></div>
</div>
<p>When a roof is not a candidate, we will say so and walk you through <a href="/services/commercial-roofing/">commercial roof repair and replacement</a> options instead.</p>

<h2>Silicone vs Acrylic vs Polyurethane Roof Coatings</h2>
<p>Each coating chemistry has strengths. Picking one is about matching it to your roof’s substrate, drainage and use — not about which product is “best” in general.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Silicone</th><th>Acrylic</th><th>Polyurethane</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Strongest at</strong></td><td>UV resistance and tolerance of ponding water</td><td>Reflectivity at a lower material cost</td><td>Impact and abrasion resistance, toughness</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Typical substrates</strong></td><td>Single-ply, modified bitumen, BUR, spray foam, metal</td><td>Metal, modified bitumen, single-ply with good slope</td><td>Metal, spray foam, roofs with foot traffic</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Ponding water</strong></td><td>Handles it well</td><td>Not recommended where water sits</td><td>Fair to good, product-dependent</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Weak spots</strong></td><td>Slippery when wet; collects dirt; limits what can be applied over it later</td><td>Can erode where water ponds; needs dry weather to cure</td><td>Strong odor during application; usually costs more</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Good fit for</strong></td><td>Flat roofs with marginal drainage</td><td>Sloped metal roofs and well-drained membranes</td><td>Roofs with heavy maintenance traffic or hail exposure</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>
<p>On many Austin roofs we combine products — for example a polyurethane base coat for toughness with a reflective top coat — following the manufacturer’s system requirements so the warranty available for that system remains valid.</p>

<h2>What Commercial Roof Coatings Fix — and What They Don’t</h2>
<h3>What a coating can solve</h3>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li>Small leaks at seams, laps and fasteners once those details are reinforced</li>
  <li>Minor membrane wear, surface cracking and UV chalking</li>
  <li>Rust on metal roofs that is cleaned and primed before coating</li>
  <li>High roof surface temperatures and heat gain into the building</li>
  <li>The need for a seamless waterproof layer over many small seams</li>
</ul>
<h3>What a coating cannot solve</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Wet insulation.</strong> It must be cut out and replaced first, or the roof keeps rotting underneath.</li>
  <li><strong>Structural problems.</strong> Sagging decks and failed framing need repair, not paint.</li>
  <li><strong>Bad drainage design.</strong> A coating can tolerate some ponding, but it does not move water to the drains.</li>
  <li><strong>A roof at the end of its life.</strong> If the membrane is detached or brittle throughout, a coating will fail with it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Our Commercial Roof Coating Process</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><h3>Assessment &amp; moisture check</h3><p>We walk the roof, photograph its condition and check for trapped moisture so we only coat what is dry and sound.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Repairs first</h3><p>Wet insulation is removed and replaced, blisters cut out, and damaged membrane, flashing or metal panels repaired.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Cleaning &amp; preparation</h3><p>The roof is pressure-washed, rust treated and primers applied where the system calls for them. Most coating failures trace back to poor prep.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Seam &amp; detail reinforcement</h3><p>Seams, fasteners, penetrations and drains get reinforcing fabric or mastic before the field coat goes on.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Coating application</h3><p>Base and top coats are applied at the manufacturer’s specified thickness, which we verify with mil gauges as we go.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Final inspection &amp; report</h3><p>We inspect the finished roof, document it with photos and give you the paperwork for any available warranty.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2>Why Coatings Make Sense on Austin Roofs</h2>
<p>Central Texas is close to an ideal place for reflective roof coatings. Long, hot summers mean rooftop equipment works hard for months, and a white, reflective coating can significantly lower roof surface temperatures compared with a dark membrane or weathered gravel. That can ease cooling loads, and it also slows the UV and heat aging that cracks older roofs.</p>
<p>Timing matters too. Most coatings need dry weather and appropriate temperatures to cure, so we schedule around spring storm season and keep an eye on the forecast. Silicone tolerates late-day humidity better than acrylic, which is one reason it is popular here. And because hail is a fact of life in Austin, <a href="/service-areas/round-rock-tx/">Round Rock</a>, <a href="/service-areas/pflugerville-tx/">Pflugerville</a> and the surrounding area, we talk through impact resistance when choosing a system — a tougher base coat or added thickness can make a difference on exposed roofs.</p>

<div class="callout"><p><strong>Is a 10–20 year extension realistic?</strong> For a roof in good condition with proper prep and the specified coating thickness, that range is typical. Many systems can also be cleaned and re-coated later rather than torn off, extending service life again. Actual results depend on the product, the roof and how well it is maintained.</p></div>

<h2>Coatings vs Replacement: A Quick Comparison</h2>
<p>A coating usually costs well below a full tear-off and replacement, creates far less noise and debris, and keeps the existing roof out of the landfill. In some cases a coating may also be treated as maintenance rather than a capital roof replacement for accounting purposes — ask your accountant how that applies to your property. Replacement wins when the roof is wet, structurally compromised or simply worn out. Use our <a href="/roofing-tools/roof-lifespan-calculator/">roof lifespan calculator</a> for a rough idea of how much life your current roof has left, then <a href="/contact-us/">book a free assessment</a> for the real answer.</p>

<h2>Coatings for Multi-Family and Mixed-Use Buildings</h2>
<p>Apartment buildings and mixed-use properties with flat roof sections are often strong coating candidates because the work is quiet compared with a tear-off and residents can stay put. We coordinate access and scheduling with property managers just as we do on our <a href="/services/multifamily-roofing/">multi-family roofing</a> projects.</p>

<p>Austin Roofing provides free coating assessments throughout the Austin metro. Call <a href="tel:+19564656045">(956) 465-6045</a> and we will tell you plainly whether your roof should be coated, repaired or replaced.</p>
